    ('h3', 'Puzzle: The Scrambled Portrait'),
    ('p', '<b>Where:</b> the corridor between the Great Hall and the Defence classroom. Offer it to whoever walks that way, or to anyone who seems stuck. It takes one of their two Lunch actions.'),
    ('read', 'Halfway down the corridor, a portrait has been sliced into nine squares and shuffled in its frame. One square is missing. A muffled voice comes from somewhere inside the mess: “Peeves! It was Peeves! Put me back, I beg you, I cannot find my own nose!”'),
    ('list', [
        'On the <b>Puzzles</b> tab, start <b>The Scrambled Portrait</b>. The picture appears on the TV.',
        'The players name a piece next to the gap; you tap it to slide it in. Talking it through together is the point.',
        'Hints, if they are stuck: “Fix the top row first, then the left column.” Then: “Leave the last two rows until the end; turn them like a wheel.” Last resort: make the next three moves for them.',
        'It is solved when pieces 1 to 8 are in order with the gap last (16 slides at best). There is no way to fail.',
    ]),
    ('read', 'The last square slides home. The old witch in the portrait blinks, touches her nose with enormous relief, and then leans out of her frame, as close as she can get. Her voice drops to a whisper. “Thank you, children. Now listen. He did not arrive on the train. He was brought. And he has been hungry for a very long time.”'),
    ('gm', 'Reward: +10 house points to each student who helped, and a tick on their spotlight note. If they ask who “he” is, she glances at the Defence classroom door, goes pale (as pale as paint can go), and says nothing more. That glance is the clue.'),

